WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.443
When you turn your own head, why does the reflex that steadies your head not fight

2
00:00:07.443 --> 00:00:08.443
you?

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:11.393
That reflex treats head motion as a disturbance.

4
00:00:12.313 --> 00:00:13.933
Your own turn would look like one.

5
00:00:14.883 --> 00:00:16.683
Does the ear tell the two apart?

6
00:00:17.543 --> 00:00:20.443
In alert monkeys, canal afferents responded to both.

7
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:23.713
So any cancelling must happen in the brain.

8
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:29.677
Roy and Cullen recorded vestibular-only neurons, thought to drive the neck reflex

9
00:00:29.677 --> 00:00:30.797
that steadies the head.

10
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:34.247
The brain has two clues to a self-made turn.

11
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:37.867
One is the motor command, which predicts the neck's feedback.

12
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:41.007
The other is the neck's actual sense of stretch.

13
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.270
Pause and predict.

14
00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.070
A passive turn. An active turn with matching neck feedback.

15
00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:50.910
An active turn with mismatched feedback.

16
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.210
Which ones drive the neuron strongly?

17
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:00.653
The passive turn did.

18
00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:03.713
So did the active turn with mismatched feedback.

19
00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:06.653
Only the matched active turn was cancelled.

20
00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:11.457
In this made-up example, nearly all of the response is cancelled.

21
00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:14.377
This is not the ear switching off.

22
00:01:15.157 --> 00:01:16.337
The afferents kept firing.

23
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:22.077
This muting may help keep the head-steadying reflex from opposing a turn you intend.

24
00:01:23.057 --> 00:01:25.497
It does not mean you stop sensing your own head movements.

25
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:28.147
A prediction must stay right.

26
00:01:29.027 --> 00:01:33.807
Brooks and colleagues recorded neurons in a cerebellar output nucleus, the rostral

27
00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:34.867
fastigial nucleus.

28
00:01:35.787 --> 00:01:38.527
Their monkeys turned their heads against a resistive load.

29
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.640
As the animals adapted, these neurons' vestibular sensitivity fell.

30
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.000
When the load was suddenly removed, it rose again.

31
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:49.143
These are recordings.

32
00:01:50.223 --> 00:01:52.963
They show what the neurons did, not that they caused the learning.

33
00:01:54.017 --> 00:01:59.077
Finally, a vestibular implant stimulates the canal branches of the nerve when both

34
00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:00.477
ears have lost their signal.

35
00:02:01.517 --> 00:02:04.457
It varies its pulses to represent head rotation.

36
00:02:05.337 --> 00:02:09.577
It does not recreate the nerve's two natural channels, and it sends no otolith

37
00:02:09.577 --> 00:02:10.577
signal.

38
00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.750
In eight adults, some balance measures improved.

39
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.050
Hearing in the implanted ear fell in all but one.

40
00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:23.113
A cohort this small shows feasibility and a trade-off, not that the device works for

41
00:02:23.113 --> 00:02:24.113
everyone.

42
00:02:24.566 --> 00:02:26.966
The nerve reports motion on two channels.

43
00:02:27.806 --> 00:02:31.946
Where neck feedback matches the prediction, these head-posture neurons pass on

44
00:02:31.946 --> 00:02:32.726
mainly the surprise.
